+# Decision: Cache Eviction Policy
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker's cache is bounded at 12 GiB and cannot hold every rendered sheet. Editors alternate between two access patterns with opposite implications for what to keep: a long sequential screening touches a large number of sheets exactly once, while a comparison session repeatedly revisits a small working set. An eviction policy tuned for one pattern degrades the other — a policy that forgets quickly loses the comparison working set to the next screening's scan; a policy that remembers too long keeps a stale working set warm after editors move to a different project. Cache entries and all replacement-policy bookkeeping are private to the preview worker (see `previews-design.md` § Architecture); no option considered here changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget, so those are background, not decision elements.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scan resistance** (major) — how well the policy keeps a comparison working set alive through a long sequential screening. This is the failure mode that made the default (recency-only) policy insufficient and the reason this decision exists.
+- **Popular-clip retention** (moderate) — how well the policy keeps a clip warm across repeated use that isn't concentrated in one session.
+- **Project-switch adaptation** (moderate) — how quickly the policy's notion of "working set" updates when editors move to a different project's clips.
+- **Bookkeeping cost** (moderate) — the per-access work and memory the policy adds beyond a plain cache lookup.
+- **Implementation and tuning complexity** (minor) — how many parameters the policy introduces that have to be chosen or tuned rather than following from the policy's logic alone.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU
+
+A single queue ordered by recency of access. Each hit moves its entry to the most-recently-used end; eviction removes from the least-recently-used end.
+
+- Scan resistance: **weak**. A long sequential screening is pure recency pressure — every sheet it touches, including ones from an active comparison working set untouched during the scan, ages toward eviction at the same rate.
+- Popular-clip retention: **partial**. A clip stays warm only as long as it keeps getting touched; history beyond the most recent access carries no weight.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **strong**. With no memory beyond recency, the working set updates immediately when access patterns change.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **strong**. A single position update per access, no counters or extra queues.
+- Implementation complexity: **strong**. No parameters beyond the cache bound itself.
+
+Summary: cheapest and most adaptive option, but defenseless against exactly the sequential-scan scenario that motivated this decision.
+
+### Frequency-based eviction with aging
+
+A per-entry hit counter, periodically decayed (aged) so that old popularity fades rather than accumulating forever. Eviction removes the lowest-count entry.
+
+- Scan resistance: **strong**. Sheets touched once during a screening accumulate low counts and are the first evicted, regardless of how recently they were touched.
+- Popular-clip retention: **strong**. A clip reused across many sessions keeps a high count independent of when it was last touched.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **weak**. A previous project's frequently-touched clips keep high counts until the aging process decays them, so they compete with the new project's working set for space rather than yielding immediately.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **weak**. Every entry needs a maintained counter, and the aging process is a periodic sweep over the cache rather than work folded into normal access.
+- Implementation complexity: **partial**. The aging interval and decay rate are additional parameters with no single obviously-correct value.
+
+Summary: the strongest retention of genuinely popular clips, paid for with counter upkeep and slowness to let go of a previous working set.
+
+### 2Q
+
+Two queues: a probationary queue (A1) for sheets rendered but not yet reused, and a main queue (Am) for sheets requested more than once. A newly completed sheet enters at A1's MRU end. A hit on a sheet in A1 promotes it to Am's MRU end; a hit on a sheet already in Am refreshes its Am MRU position. Eviction drains A1's LRU end first, only reaching into Am's LRU end once A1 is empty. This is the two-queue variant without an A1-out ghost list — a sheet evicted from Am loses its reuse history and re-enters at A1 if requested again, rather than being fast-tracked back into Am.
+
+- Scan resistance: **strong**. A sequential screening's one-off sheets live and die entirely within A1; they never reach Am and so can never evict something that's there.
+- Popular-clip retention: **partial**. Am protects a sheet for as long as Am itself isn't under eviction pressure, but because this variant carries no ghost list, a sheet evicted from Am during sustained Am pressure loses its history and must requalify from A1 — unlike aging's counters, which persist that history indefinitely.
+- Project-switch adaptation: **strong**. Am membership depends on recent reuse rather than an accumulated counter, so a new project's working set earns its way into Am the same way LRU adapts, rather than competing with aged-but-not-yet-decayed counts from the old one.
+- Bookkeeping cost: **partial**. Two queues and a promote-or-refresh decision per access — more than LRU's single position update, but no periodic sweep over the whole cache the way aging requires.
+- Implementation complexity: **partial**. Introduces one parameter with no self-evident value: the size split between A1 and Am (left open; see `previews-design.md` § Open Questions & Future Decisions).
+
+Summary: trades two queues and a size-split parameter for scan resistance, without inheriting frequency-aging's slowness to forget a previous working set.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: 2Q.**
+
+Scan resistance is the major criterion, and it is also literally the criterion this decision exists to settle — the default recency-only behavior let a long sequential screening evict the comparison sheets editors were mid-task on, which is what forced this decision past the point LRU could resolve it on its own. 2Q and frequency-aging both score strong here; between them, 2Q wins on project-switch adaptation (strong vs. weak) without conceding much on popular-clip retention (partial vs. strong) — aging's advantage there is bought by keeping a departed project's favorites warm longer than the editors who created them are still looking at them, which is a worse failure mode for this workload than occasionally having to re-promote a sheet that fell out of Am.
+
+The cost accepted is bookkeeping and implementation complexity (both moderate/minor, both only partial for 2Q): two queues instead of one, and a size-split parameter with no formula to derive it from — it's deferred in the LLD for tuning against observed traffic rather than fixed here. The no-ghost-list variant is deliberate, not an oversight: it keeps the design to the two queues actually discussed, accepting that a sheet evicted from Am during sustained Am pressure has to requalify through A1 rather than being remembered indefinitely. If that requalification cost turns out to matter in practice, a ghost list (2Q's classic third queue) is the natural extension — but it is not part of this decision.
